 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Linear Bounding Volume Hierarchy (LBVH) for Volume Rendering](11217-linear-bounding-volume-hierarchy-lbvh-for-volume-rendering.md)
-A hierarchical data structure for sparse volume rendering with empty space skipping, adapted from surface ray tracing LBVH to uniform grids, targeting GPU implementation via CUDA.
+Linear Bounding Volume Hierarchy (LBVH) is a hierarchical data structure for sparse volume rendering that enables empty space skipping, adapted from surface ray tracing to uniform grids and targeted for GPU implementation via CUDA. It sets itself apart from SAH-based methods (such as k-d trees) by using a middle-split heuristic on Morton-ordered bricks, offering significantly superior construction performance at the cost of inferior culling properties.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** Allows constructing the acceleration structure on the GPU at interactive frame rates for $1024^3$ volumes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Rendering Speed:** The resulting LBVH structure allows for two to three times higher frame rates than using no empty-space skipping during ray marching [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient GPU construction; significant speedup for sparse data.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires uniform grid partitioning; performance depends on sparsity.
